==Abstract==

This document specifies '''Mnemonic Key''' (MK), a codex32-derived backup format for individual extended public keys (xpubs). An MK-encoded backup carries one BIP 32 xpub plus its origin metadata (master fingerprint and derivation path), packaged with one or more wallet-policy-template ID stubs for indexing against companion Mnemonic Descriptor (MD) policy cards. MK is designed for the foreign-xpub multisig recovery use case: each cosigner backs up their own xpub on its own card, separate from the policy card and from other cosigners' xpubs. Together with [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/blob/main/bip/bip-mnemonic-descriptor.mediawiki MD] and [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0093.mediawiki BIP 93] (codex32 seed shares), MK forms a triad of codex32-derived backup formats covering wallet structure, keys, and seed material respectively.

==Motivation==

[https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/blob/main/bip/bip-mnemonic-descriptor.mediawiki MD] (HRP <code>md1</code>) encodes only the BIP 388 wallet-policy template (the <code>@N</code>-placeholder shape), not the xpubs that map placeholders to keys. For wallets where the user holds every seed referenced by the policy, MD's omission is fine — the seeds reconstruct the xpubs, and the descriptor is reconstructable from policy template plus seeds. For '''foreign-xpub multisig''' (the user is one of N cosigners and does not hold all seeds), MD alone is insufficient: the missing xpubs must come from somewhere at recovery time.

Existing wallet formats (Coldcard <code>.txt</code> multisig configs, Sparrow <code>.json</code>, Liana descriptor files) bundle xpubs and policy together. MK takes a different shape: separate per-cosigner cards, optimized for engraving on durable physical media. Reasons:

* '''Foreign-xpub-only by default.''' Wallets where the user holds all seeds don't need MK at all.
* '''Atomic per-cosigner cards.''' Lose one card = lose one cosigner's xpub. With a bundle card, lose one card = lose every cosigner's xpub.
* '''Reusability.''' One cosigner's xpub backed up on one MK card serves every wallet that cosigner participates in. No re-engraving when joining a new multisig.
* '''Trust boundary alignment.''' A cosigner can hand their MK card to the wallet creator without revealing other cosigners' xpubs.

MK reuses BIP 93's BCH plumbing (same generator polynomials, same alphabet, same chunking infrastructure as codex32 and MD), with HRP-mixing and a per-format target residue providing domain separation. The cryptographic guarantees of codex32 — error correction of up to 4 character substitutions for '''both''' the regular and long codes — apply to MK without modification. (8 is the long code's error-''detection'' radius, not its correction radius; both codes correct up to <code>t = 4</code>.)

==Definitions and notation==

* '''Key card''' or '''MK card''': a codex32-derived string with HRP <code>mk</code>, encoding one xpub plus its metadata.
* '''xpub''': a BIP 32 extended public key. The full 78-byte serialized form is the binary representation of a Base58Check-decoded xpub. MK encodes a 73-byte ''compact'' form on the wire (see §"xpub encoding"); the full form is reconstructed at decode time.
* '''Origin fingerprint''': the 4-byte master-key fingerprint identifying the seed from which the xpub was derived. Verbatim from BIP 380 origin notation <code>[fp/...]</code>. Optional in MK v0.1; presence is signaled by the bytecode header's fingerprint flag.
* '''Origin path''': the BIP 32 derivation path from the master seed to the xpub. E.g., <code>m/48'/0'/0'/2'</code>.
* '''Policy ID''' (a.k.a. '''WalletPolicyId'''): the 16-byte canonical-''expanded'' policy identity <code>md_codec::compute_wallet_policy_id(descriptor)</code> (md SPEC v0.13 §5.3), an encoder-divergence-free hash of an MD-encoded policy ''template'' — placeholders + shared path, not the concrete cosigner xpubs (a one-to-many relationship to actual wallets, not a wallet instance). It '''supersedes''' the earlier v0.8.0 "Policy ID" = <code>SHA-256(canonical_bytecode)[0..16]</code> (the encoding-sensitive md1 bytecode hash <code>Md1EncodingId</code>, itself renamed from "wallet ID" in [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/releases/tag/md-codec-v0.8.0 md-codec v0.8.0]); the bytecode hash predated WalletPolicyId and is not stable under re-encoding the same logical wallet. See the naming note below (audit I1, 2026-06-10).
* '''Policy ID stub''': the top 4 bytes of a Policy ID. Carried by MK cards as an indexing aid.
* '''Wallet Instance ID''': the derived 16-byte value <code>SHA-256(canonical_bytecode || canonical_xpub_serialization)[0..16]</code> uniquely identifying a wallet instance — i.e., a template plus a specific cosigner xpub set. Defined in [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/releases/tag/md-codec-v0.8.0 md-codec v0.8.0]'s BIP for cross-tool consistency. Recovery tools compute it on demand from policy + assembled xpubs.
* '''<code>chunk_set_id</code>''': a 20-bit per-encoding random tag carried in the chunked-string header, used for reassembly mismatch detection. Each chunk of a multi-chunk MK card carries the same <code>chunk_set_id</code>; a decoder rejects assemblies with mismatched values. (md-codec v0.8.x originally called this field "wallet identifier"; [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/releases/tag/md-codec-v0.9.0 md-codec v0.9.0] renamed it to <code>chunk_set_id</code> across both the BIP draft and the reference implementation. Wire format unchanged.)

===Naming note (Policy ID vs Wallet Instance ID, and <code>chunk_set_id</code>)===

The 16-byte hash MD computes as <code>SHA-256(canonical_bytecode)[0..16]</code> covers the BIP 388 wallet-policy ''template'' only — placeholders and shared path, not the concrete cosigner xpubs. Two distinct wallets that share an identical template (same multisig shape, different cosigner sets) collide on this value. md-codec versions ≤ 0.7.x called this value "wallet ID" — a one-to-one name for a one-to-many relationship that confused readers. MK's design exposed the issue, and [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/releases/tag/md-codec-v0.8.0 md-codec v0.8.0] ships the resolution:

# Renamed the existing 16-byte value to '''Policy ID'''. The 16-byte value, the 4-byte stub, and the 12-word BIP 39 anchor encoding are byte-identical to v0.7.x; the rename is naming-only.
# Defined a new derived '''Wallet Instance ID''' = <code>SHA-256(canonical_bytecode || canonical_xpub_serialization)[0..16]</code> as a recovery-time computation, documented in the MD BIP but not carried by any physical card.
# '''md-codec v0.13 (audit I1, 2026-06-10):''' the Policy ID — and therefore the 4-byte stub — is now the '''WalletPolicyId''' (<code>compute_wallet_policy_id</code>), a canonical-''expanded'' hash that is stable under re-encoding the same logical wallet (origin/use-site elision, override-vs-baseline path placement). This is a '''value''' change scoped to the linkage primitive: it '''supersedes''' the v0.8.0 bytecode-hash Policy ID, so a stub computed under the old <code>SHA-256(canonical_bytecode)</code> formula no longer matches. The byte-identity to v0.7.x noted above held only through the v0.8.0 naming-only rename; v0.13 changes the value. (The Wallet Instance ID is unaffected.)
# This BIP uses the renamed nomenclature throughout and treats Policy ID stubs as a template-level indexing filter, with Wallet Instance ID as the cryptographic per-instance check at descriptor-reassembly time.

A separate naming-only rename — md-codec's chunked-string-header field "wallet identifier" → '''<code>chunk_set_id</code>''' — landed in [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/releases/tag/md-codec-v0.9.0 md-codec v0.9.0] (docs-and-symbols-only; wire format unchanged). MK uses the renamed name <code>chunk_set_id</code> throughout this BIP and in the reference implementation, in agreement with the md1 BIP draft as published.

==Format overview==

An MK card is a codex32-derived string of the form:

<pre>
  mk1 <data> <BCH checksum>
</pre>

The data section, after chunk-header reassembly, is a payload encoding (per closure-locked field order; see §"Payload field order" for normative ordering):

<pre>
  [bytecode_header   : 1 byte]
  [stub_count        : 1 byte; MUST be ≥ 1]
  [policy_id_stub_1 ... policy_id_stub_N : 4 × N bytes]
  [origin_fingerprint: 4 bytes]   ← present iff bytecode_header bit 2 set
  [origin_path       : variable; 1 byte std-table indicator OR 2..=52 B explicit-path escape]
  [xpub_compact      : 73 bytes]
</pre>

Total bytes for a typical 1-stub mainnet card with std-table indicator and fingerprint present: <code>1 + 1 + 4 + 4 + 1 + 73 = 84 B</code>.

The single-string capacity of long-code codex32 is 56 bytes payload (per the chunk-length envelope below). The smallest valid v0.1 mk1 bytecode (1-byte header + 1-byte stub_count + 4-byte single stub + std-table path indicator + 73-byte compact xpub, fingerprint omitted) is <code>1 + 1 + 4 + 1 + 73 = 80</code> bytes — already above the 56-byte cap. '''Every conforming v0.1 mk1 KeyCard therefore encodes as a chunked card''' (the typical 1-stub-with-fingerprint case is 84 bytes; with the 4-byte cross-chunk hash appended, the 88-byte stream splits into '''2 chunks — chunk 0 long-code, chunk 1 regular-code'''; a mixed-code chunk set is the normal shape, see §"Chunking (fragment split)"). The <code>SingleString</code> chunk-type variant is reserved for future format extensions whose bytecode could shrink below 56 bytes; it is wire-defined for forward compatibility but unreachable for v0.1 encoders.

==Specification==

===Encoding layer===

====Human-readable part====

The HRP is <code>mk</code> (lowercase, two characters). Per BIP 173, the separator <code>1</code> follows, giving the prefix <code>mk1</code>. HRP case MUST match the case of the data part. Mixed case within a single string is invalid and MUST be rejected.

====BCH plumbing====

MK reuses [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0093.mediawiki BIP 93]'s generator polynomials verbatim. Domain separation between MK, [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/blob/main/bip/bip-mnemonic-descriptor.mediawiki MD], and codex32 is provided by:

# '''HRP-mixing''' (BIP 173-style HRP expansion folded into the polymod's initial state).
# '''Per-format target residue constants''', NUMS-derived from a per-format domain string.

For MK:

<pre>
  domain_string         = b"shibbolethnumskey"   (17 bytes ASCII)
  MK_REGULAR_CONST      = 0x1062435f91072fa5c    (65 bits)
  MK_LONG_CONST         = 0x41890d7e441cbe97273  (75 bits)
</pre>

The domain string differs from MD's <code>"shibbolethnums"</code> (and codex32's domain). Independent NUMS strings produce essentially uncorrelated 65/75-bit residues, so cross-format false-positive resistance is preserved.

====Why new target constants?====

Derivation procedure (NUMS — "Nothing Up My Sleeve"). The string itself is the audit trail; any reader can recompute the SHA-256 and verify that the constants follow.

<source lang="python">
import hashlib
h = hashlib.sha256(b"shibbolethnumskey").digest()
MK_REGULAR_CONST = int.from_bytes(h, 'big') >> (256 - 65)  # = 0x1062435f91072fa5c
MK_LONG_CONST    = int.from_bytes(h, 'big') >> (256 - 75)  # = 0x41890d7e441cbe97273
</source>

Cross-check: applying the same procedure to <code>b"shibbolethnums"</code> reproduces MD's published <code>T_REGULAR = 0x0815c07747a3392e7</code> and the long-generator target <code>0x205701dd1e8ce4b9f47</code> (the latter was MD's <code>T_LONG</code> before MD retired its long code; MK retains a long code and derives its own <code>MK_LONG_CONST</code> by this same procedure with HRP <code>"mk"</code>), confirming derivation correctness.

We chose new constants rather than reusing BIP 93's <code>MS32_CONST</code> values for two reasons. First, although HRP-mixing produces residues that differ between any two HRPs sharing this polynomial, picking distinct target constants provides residue-level separation as well: damage that maps an MK string toward codex32-style validity is statistically prevented by the combination. Second, signaling matters — reusing codex32's specific target constant verbatim would invite the misreading that MK is "codex32 with a different HRP" rather than a distinct format that borrows codex32's polynomial.

The reproducible NUMS derivation above, together with the embedded conformance vectors (§"Test Vectors") — real MK cards that decode '''only''' under the specified target constants — provides the structural-relationship evidence at the same reproducer-in-BIP bar the sibling [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/blob/main/bip/bip-mnemonic-descriptor.mediawiki MD] BIP adopted. A formal '''structural-relationship audit''' of these constants against the BIP 93 BCH polynomial — machine-verifying weight-distribution properties under the new target and confirming no accidental codeword-space intersection with MD or codex32 — remains a documented, non-blocking pre-submission item; it is not a v0.1 release blocker.

====Checksum====

MK's checksum is a BCH error-correcting code with BIP 173-style HRP-mixing. It is '''init- and algorithm-identical to [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/blob/main/bip/bip-mnemonic-descriptor.mediawiki MD]'s §"Checksum"''': MK shares MD's polymod initial state and BIP 93's generator polynomials verbatim; only the HRP (<code>"mk"</code>), the per-format target constants (<code>MK_REGULAR_CONST</code> / <code>MK_LONG_CONST</code>, above), and code-variant availability differ (MK retains both a regular and a long code; MD has only the regular code). The polymod is BIP 93's <code>ms32_polymod</code> (regular code) or <code>ms32_long_polymod</code> (long code). This section is fully normative: an independent implementation MUST reproduce it byte-for-byte, or it will reject every real MK card.

=====Polymod initial state=====

The polymod is seeded with the constellation-shared initial residue

<pre>
  POLYMOD_INIT = 0x23181b3
</pre>

'''before''' processing any input symbol. This is '''not''' <code>1</code> — the generic BIP 173 <code>bech32_polymod</code> start state, and the seed the <code>ms1</code> reference uses (with an <code>hrp_expand("ms")</code> prepend) to stay bit-compatible with the external <code>rust-codex32</code> engine. It '''is''' the exact initial residue of BIP 93's published <code>ms32_polymod</code>/<code>ms32_long_polymod</code> (<code>residue = 0x23181b3</code>, which pre-folds codex32's fixed HRP <code>"ms"</code>; BIP 93's caller prepends no HRP expansion). MK and MD share this seed verbatim. The choice is safe because each code (regular and long) is self-contained: the same init seeds both checksum '''creation''' and '''verification''', so its contribution cancels and a valid codeword's residue equals its per-HRP target at every length, for any fixed init. Domain separation between MK, MD, and codex32 is carried entirely by the per-HRP target constants and the HRP expansion — never by this init.

Equivalence note (for readers cross-checking against codex32): <code>0x23181b3</code> is itself the residue obtained by folding <code>hrp_expand("ms")</code> into an init of <code>1</code>. MK's checksum input is therefore equivalent to <code>hrp_expand("ms") || hrp_expand("mk") || data</code> processed from an init of <code>1</code> — but a conforming implementation MUST simply seed <code>0x23181b3</code> and prepend <code>hrp_expand("mk")</code>, exactly as specified here. Do '''not''' seed from <code>1</code>, and do '''not''' seed from the fold of <code>hrp_expand("mk")</code> alone: both readings reject every real MK card (see the worked example below).

=====HRP expansion=====

<code>hrp_expand("mk")</code> is computed exactly as in BIP 173 §"Bech32": for each HRP character <code>c</code> emit <code>ord(c) >> 5</code>; emit a single zero; then for each HRP character emit <code>ord(c) & 31</code>. For the lowercase HRP <code>"mk"</code> this yields the five 5-bit values:

<pre>
  hrp_expand("mk") = [3, 3, 0, 13, 11]
</pre>

Derivation: <code>ord('m') = 109 = 0x6d</code>, so <code>109 >> 5 = 3</code> and <code>109 & 31 = 13</code>. <code>ord('k') = 107 = 0x6b</code>, so <code>107 >> 5 = 3</code> and <code>107 & 31 = 11</code>. The separator zero falls between the high-half list <code>[3, 3]</code> and the low-half list <code>[13, 11]</code>. (Length = 2 × len(HRP) + 1 = 5.)

=====Generator polynomials=====

The five per-step feedback constants are BIP 93's, verbatim. The regular code carries a 65-bit residue (top 5 bits select the feedback terms; the low 60 bits are the running state); the long code carries a 75-bit residue (top 5 bits select; low 70 bits state):

<pre>
  # Regular code — shift = 60, mask = 0x0fffffffffffffff
  GEN_REGULAR = [
    0x19dc500ce73fde210,
    0x1bfae00def77fe529,
    0x1fbd920fffe7bee52,
    0x1739640bdeee3fdad,
    0x07729a039cfc75f5a,
  ]

  # Long code — shift = 70, mask = 0x3fffffffffffffffff
  GEN_LONG = [
    0x3d59d273535ea62d897,
    0x7a9becb6361c6c51507,
    0x543f9b7e6c38d8a2a0e,
    0x0c577eaeccf1990d13c,
    0x1887f74f8dc71b10651,
  ]
</pre>

=====Algorithm=====

<source lang="python">
POLYMOD_INIT = 0x23181b3

def polymod(values, gen, shift, mask):
    # values: the full sequence of 5-bit symbols to fold in.
    residue = POLYMOD_INIT
    for v in values:
        b = residue >> shift
        residue = ((residue & mask) << 5) ^ v
        for i in range(5):
            if (b >> i) & 1:
                residue ^= gen[i]
    return residue

def hrp_expand(hrp):
    return [ord(c) >> 5 for c in hrp] + [0] + [ord(c) & 31 for c in hrp]
</source>

Let <code>(GEN, SHIFT, MASK, TARGET, k)</code> be <code>(GEN_REGULAR, 60, 0x0fffffffffffffff, MK_REGULAR_CONST, 13)</code> for the regular code or <code>(GEN_LONG, 70, 0x3fffffffffffffffff, MK_LONG_CONST, 15)</code> for the long code.

To '''create''' a checksum for a data part <code>data</code> (the sequence of 5-bit header + payload symbols, without a checksum):

# Choose the code by length (see "Code selection", below).
# Compute <code>P = polymod(hrp_expand("mk") || data || [0]*k, GEN, SHIFT, MASK) XOR TARGET</code>.
# Extract the <code>k</code> checksum symbols from <code>P</code> in big-endian 5-bit order: symbol <code>i</code> (for <code>i = 0 .. k−1</code>) is <code>(P >> (5 * (k − 1 − i))) & 0x1F</code>. The '''first''' checksum symbol is the top 5 bits of the XORed residue <code>P</code>; the last is its low 5 bits.
# Append the <code>k</code> symbols to <code>data</code> to form the full data part.

To '''verify''' a full data part <code>data_with_checksum</code> (header + payload + the trailing 13/15 checksum symbols), the string is valid iff

<pre>
  polymod(hrp_expand("mk") || data_with_checksum, GEN, SHIFT, MASK) == TARGET
</pre>

Error correction operates on the same residue: <code>polymod(hrp_expand("mk") || data_with_checksum) XOR TARGET</code> is congruent to the error polynomial modulo the generator, and is fed to the syndrome-based decoder (§"Error-correction guarantees").

=====Code selection (length thresholds)=====

The regular and long codes are selected by data-part length; the encoder and decoder sides MUST agree. A "≤ 93 → regular, else long" shortcut is '''wrong''' (vector V1's chunk 0 has a 93-symbol pre-checksum data part that takes the '''long''' code — <code>93 + 15 = 108</code> — not the regular code):

* '''Encoder.''' Given a pre-checksum data part of <code>d</code> 5-bit symbols: if <code>d + 13</code> lies in <code>14..=93</code>, use the regular code (append 13 checksum symbols); else if <code>d + 15</code> lies in <code>96..=108</code>, use the long code (append 15); otherwise the data part is unencodable — too short, in the reserved 94–95 gap, or too long for v0.1 (<code>Error::InvalidStringLength</code>). Equivalently: pre-checksum data of 1–80 symbols → regular, 81–93 → long, > 93 invalid.
* '''Decoder.''' Given a received data part (payload + checksum) of total length <code>L</code>: <code>L</code> in <code>14..=93</code> → regular code; <code>L</code> in <code>94..=95</code> → '''reserved-invalid''' (<code>Error::InvalidStringLength</code>); <code>L</code> in <code>96..=108</code> → long code; any other <code>L</code> → invalid (<code>Error::InvalidStringLength</code>). The minimum valid data-part length is 14 symbols; there is no <code>L < 14</code> valid string.

=====Worked example (vector V1)=====

Vector <code>V1_bip48_mainnet_1_stub_with_fp</code> (§"Test Vectors") is a 1-stub mainnet BIP 48 segwit-v0 multisig card with the fingerprint present. Its 84-byte canonical bytecode is:

<pre>
  040111223344aabbccdd050488b21e10203001
  abababababababababababababababababababababababababababababababab
  031b84c5567b126440995d3ed5aaba0565d71e1834604819ff9c17f5e9d5dd078f
</pre>

decomposing (per §"Payload field order") as:

<pre>
  04                                 bytecode header (v0, fingerprint flag set)
  01                                 stub_count = 1
  11223344                           policy_id_stub_1
  aabbccdd                           origin_fingerprint
  05                                 origin_path indicator (m/48'/0'/0'/2')
  0488b21e                           xpub.version (mainnet)
  10203001                           xpub.parent_fingerprint
  abab..ab  (32 bytes)               xpub.chain_code
  03 1b84..078f (33 bytes)           xpub.public_key (compressed)
</pre>

'''Chunking.''' <code>cross_chunk_hash = SHA-256(bytecode)[0..4] = 83bb262d</code>; the split stream is <code>bytecode || 83bb262d</code> = 88 bytes. With a 53-byte fragment target this splits into two fragments: chunk 0 = bytes 0..53 (53 bytes) → long code; chunk 1 = bytes 53..88 (35 bytes) → regular code. The per-encoding <code>chunk_set_id = 0x12345</code> (74565).

'''Chunk 0 (long code).''' The 8-symbol chunked header is <code>[0, 1, 2, 8, 26, 5, 1, 0]</code> = <code>version 0</code>, <code>type 1</code> (Chunked), <code>chunk_set_id 0x12345</code> as four big-endian 5-bit symbols <code>[2, 8, 26, 5]</code>, <code>total_chunks−1 = 1</code>, <code>chunk_index 0</code> — bech32 <code>"qpzg69pq"</code>. The 53 fragment bytes convert (8→5-bit, MSB-first, zero-padded) to 85 symbols, giving a 93-symbol pre-checksum data part; <code>93 + 15 = 108</code> → long code. The 15 checksum symbols computed by the algorithm above are <code>[6, 5, 7, 18, 30, 26, 27, 21, 27, 18, 31, 14, 1, 23, 9]</code> = bech32 <code>"x98j76m4mjlwphf"</code>, and <code>polymod(hrp_expand("mk") || data_with_checksum, GEN_LONG, 70, 0x3fffffffffffffffff) == MK_LONG_CONST</code>. The full string is:

<pre>
  mk1qpzg69pqqsq3zg3ngj4thnxaq5zg3vs7zqsrqqdt4w46h2at4w46h2at4w46h2at4w46h2at4w46h2at4w46h2at4vp3kx98j76m4mjlwphf
</pre>

'''Chunk 1 (regular code).''' The 8-symbol chunked header is <code>[0, 1, 2, 8, 26, 5, 1, 1]</code> (same as chunk 0 but <code>chunk_index 1</code>) — bech32 <code>"qpzg69pp"</code>. The 35 fragment bytes convert to exactly 56 symbols (280 bits, no padding), giving a 64-symbol pre-checksum data part; <code>64 + 13 = 77</code> → regular code. The 13 checksum symbols are <code>[1, 28, 8, 10, 27, 27, 18, 11, 9, 25, 31, 26, 6]</code> = bech32 <code>"pug2mmjtfel6x"</code>, and <code>polymod(hrp_expand("mk") || data_with_checksum, GEN_REGULAR, 60, 0x0fffffffffffffff) == MK_REGULAR_CONST</code>. The full string is:

<pre>
  mk1qpzg69ppsnz4v7cjv3qfjhf76k4t5pt96u0psdrqfqvll8qh7h5athg837pmkf3dpug2mmjtfel6x
</pre>

Both strings above are the exact published V1 strings. A mixed-code chunk set (chunk 0 long, chunk 1 regular) is the '''normal''' shape for a v0.1 MK card — see §"Chunking (fragment split)".

====Alphabet====

Same bech32 alphabet as BIP 93 / BIP 173 / [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/blob/main/bip/bip-mnemonic-descriptor.mediawiki MD]:

<pre>
  qpzry9x8gf2tvdw0s3jn54khce6mua7l
</pre>

The alphabet excludes <code>1</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>i</code>, <code>o</code>, and uppercase variants thereof to avoid common transcription confusables.

====Length envelope====

Same chunk-length conventions as MD (mirroring [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/blob/main/bip/bip-mnemonic-descriptor.mediawiki MD §"Length envelope"]):

* '''Regular code''' (BCH(93, 80)): total data lengths up to and including 93 characters; 13-character checksum. Capacity: 48 bytes single-string payload, 45 bytes per chunked fragment.
* '''Long code''' (BCH(108, 93)): total data lengths from 96 through 108 characters; 15-character checksum. Capacity: 56 bytes single-string payload, 53 bytes per chunked fragment.

Total data length 94–95 is invalid and MUST be rejected.

Maximum 32 chunks per MK card. With long-code chunked encoding plus the 4-byte cross-chunk integrity hash, a card can encode up to <code>32 × 53 − 4 = 1692</code> bytes of canonical bytecode — vastly above any plausible MK payload.

====Error-correction guarantees====

Both codes are BCH codes with minimum distance ≥ 9 (which is what permits both t = 4 substitution correction and detection of any error pattern affecting up to 8 characters; "8" is the detection radius, '''not''' the minimum distance). The normative correction guarantee is:

* '''Correction of up to 4 character substitutions''' (both codes; the full <code>t = 4</code> capacity of BCH(93,80) and BCH(108,93)). Conformant decoders MUST correct any received string within Hamming distance 4 of a valid codeword.

Detection and erasure behaviour follow from the minimum distance and are informative code-capability notes, '''not''' v0.1 conformance requirements:

* Detection of any error pattern affecting up to 8 characters within a single string (a detection radius of 8, following from the minimum distance ≥ 9).
* The code distance would in principle permit correction of up to 8 erasures (positions known to be damaged), or up to 13 (regular) / 15 (long) consecutive erasures (burst damage), if an erasure-aware decoder were implemented. '''The mk-codec reference implements no erasure decoding''': <code>decode_string</code> rejects any non-alphabet character (<code>Error::InvalidChar</code>), so an erasure position cannot even be marked on input. Erasure and burst correction are therefore capabilities of the code, not guarantees of any conforming v0.1 decoder. See FOLLOWUP <code>impl-bch-erasure-decoding-md-mk</code>.

Detection of error patterns affecting more than 8 characters is probabilistic. The expected probability that a randomly damaged string of more than 8 errors decodes to a valid codeword is bounded by approximately 2<sup>−65</sup> (regular code) or 2<sup>−75</sup> (long code).

Decoders SHOULD report which correction was applied. The reference decoder reports one of <code>clean</code> / <code>N substitutions corrected</code> / <code>failed</code> (<code>Error::BchUncorrectable</code>); the richer states named in the sibling [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/blob/main/bip/bip-mnemonic-descriptor.mediawiki MD §"Decoder reporting"] — <code>N erasures corrected</code>, <code>structure-aided</code> / guided recovery, and confidence-tier reporting — are '''informative and unimplemented in mk-codec v0.1'''; they are not conformance requirements. See FOLLOWUPs <code>impl-guided-recovery-md-mk</code> and <code>impl-confidence-tier-reporting-md-mk</code>. This downgrade is symmetric with the sibling MD BIP.

====String-layer header====

Mirrors MD exactly. Two variants:

'''Single-string header''' (2 chars):

<pre>
  char 0:  version       (5 bits)   — 0x0 in v0.1; decoders MUST reject unknown versions
  char 1:  type = 0x00   (5 bits)   — SingleString
</pre>

'''Chunked header''' (8 chars):

<pre>
  char 0:    version          (5 bits)
  char 1:    type = 0x01      (5 bits)   — Chunked
  chars 2-5: chunk_set_id     (20 bits, random per-encoding)
  char 6:    total_chunks     (5 bits, semantic range 1..=32, encoded as count − 1)
  char 7:    chunk_index      (5 bits, range 0..total_chunks)
</pre>

'''Wire encoding for <code>total_chunks</code>:''' the 5-bit field carries <code>count − 1</code> so the semantic range <code>1..=32</code> maps onto the wire range <code>0..=31</code>. Encoders MUST emit <code>count − 1</code>; decoders MUST add 1 after reading the 5-bit field. (A literal "1..=32" wire encoding is impossible because a 5-bit field holds 32 distinct values whose minimum is 0, not 1.)

'''Wire encoding for <code>chunk_set_id</code>:''' the 20-bit value is packed into chars 2–5 in big-endian 5-bit-symbol order — bits 19..15 in char 2, 14..10 in char 3, 9..5 in char 4, 4..0 in char 5. Decoders reconstruct the value via <code>(char2 &lt;&lt; 15) | (char3 &lt;&lt; 10) | (char4 &lt;&lt; 5) | char5</code>.

<code>chunk_set_id</code> is opaque to the format; its only purpose is mismatch detection during reassembly within a single chunk set. Decoders MUST accept any 20-bit value; the field is not subject to interop validation beyond the within-set mismatch check.

Encoders generating fresh cards MAY draw <code>chunk_set_id</code> from a cryptographically secure random source. Encoders producing reproducible output across cold runs (engraving toolkits, regression-pinned vector corpora, deterministic test fixtures) SHOULD instead derive <code>chunk_set_id</code> from a stable cross-card binding identifier — for example, the leading 20 bits of the first <code>policy_id_stub</code>:

<pre>
  chunk_set_id = (stub[0] << 12) | (stub[1] << 4) | (stub[2] >> 4)
</pre>

Either choice is interop-equivalent — the field is opaque — and the deterministic option lets downstream toolkits SHA-pin their fixture corpora.

Note (toolkit slot-XOR): the reference bundling toolkit derives per-card <code>chunk_set_id</code> values by XORing the cosigner '''slot index''' into the derivation, so that distinct cosigner cards in one bundle get distinct <code>chunk_set_id</code>s. The plain formula above therefore reproduces the toolkit's value only for '''slot 0'''; for slot <code>s</code> the toolkit's value is the formula XOR a slot-derived term. This does not affect interop (the field is opaque and only mismatch-checked within a single card's chunk set), but a reader reproducing a specific toolkit-emitted card byte-for-byte must apply the same slot XOR.

Chunk-type byte assignments:

{| class="wikitable"
! Type byte !! Variant !! Use
|-
| <code>0x00</code> || <code>SingleString</code> || Payload + headers fit in one long-code string.
|-
| <code>0x01</code> || <code>Chunked</code> || Default for typical MK cards.
|-
| <code>0x02..0x1F</code> || reserved || Decoders MUST reject; range exhausts the 5-bit field.
|}

The <code>type</code> field is exactly 5 bits wide; the reserved range covers all unallocated values without spillover space. Future format extensions cannot widen the field without bumping the version byte.

====Cross-chunk integrity hash====

For chunked cards, the canonical bytecode is followed by a 4-byte cross-chunk integrity hash before chunk-splitting:

<pre>
  cross_chunk_hash = SHA-256(canonical_bytecode)[0..4]
</pre>

The hash is the first 4 bytes of the SHA-256 of the canonical bytecode (everything from the bytecode header through the end of the xpub_compact field). The hash is appended to the canonical bytecode, and the resulting byte stream is split into chunk fragments.

Decoders compute the hash over the reassembled bytecode (excluding the trailing 4 hash bytes) and verify it matches the trailing bytes. Mismatch MUST cause the decode to fail with an error specifically identifying the cross-chunk integrity check (<code>CrossChunkHashMismatch</code>).

====Chunking (fragment split)====

Because the smallest conforming v0.1 mk1 bytecode (80 bytes) already exceeds the 56-byte single-string capacity (§"Format overview"), every v0.1 card is chunked. The split below is '''normative for byte-identical reproduction'''; decoders MUST accept any valid division that reassembles to the same stream (see below).

Encoder procedure:

# Form the stream <code>bytecode || cross_chunk_hash</code> (the 4-byte cross-chunk hash appended, above).
# Split the stream into successive '''53-byte''' fragments (<code>CHUNKED_FRAGMENT_LONG_BYTES</code>). Every fragment but the last is exactly 53 bytes; the last fragment is the remainder (1..=53 bytes). The number of chunks is <code>ceil(stream_len / 53)</code>, capped at 32 (bytecodes above <code>32 × 53 − 4 = 1692</code> bytes are rejected with <code>Error::CardPayloadTooLarge</code>).
# For each fragment, build the chunk's data part as the 8-symbol chunked header (§"String-layer header") followed by the fragment's bytes repacked 8→5-bit (MSB-first, with the final 5-bit symbol zero-padded if the fragment's bit count is not a multiple of 5).
# BCH-checksum each chunk's data part with the code variant '''auto-selected by that chunk's data-part length''' (§"Checksum", code selection). A full 53-byte fragment yields a 93-symbol data part → long code; a shorter final fragment may yield a regular-code data part. '''Mixed-code chunk sets (some long, some regular) are the normal shape''' — do not assume all chunks share a code variant.

Decoders MUST accept '''any''' byte-level fragment division that reassembles — concatenating the per-fragment bytes in <code>chunk_index</code> order — to the same <code>bytecode || cross_chunk_hash</code> stream, then re-verifies the cross-chunk hash. The 53-byte fragment size is the encoder's emit policy, not a decode-time constraint.

'''Contrast with MD (do not cross-import).''' MK and MD both frame chunks at '''byte''' boundaries — neither uses sub-byte / bit-boundary framing. They differ in the '''encoder emit policy''': MK emits a '''fixed 53-byte''' fragment per chunk (one xpub-sized record, each independently 8→5-bit repacked and checksummed), whereas MD emits '''variable, near-equal whole-byte slices''' sized from the assembled payload and a per-chunk bit budget (MD BIP §"Chunking"; MD's split is deterministic, not encoder-chosen, and is '''not''' a fixed 53-byte size). An MK implementation MUST use the fixed 53-byte emit policy; applying MD's variable near-equal-slice sizing to MK (or MK's fixed 53-byte size to MD) produces non-reproducible strings.

====Payload byte-alignment====

Decoders MUST reject any MK string whose 5-bit payload symbols, after successful BCH checksum verification, do not byte-align — i.e., where the trailing pad bits of the final 5-bit symbol are non-zero. Such inputs are well-formed at the BCH layer but cannot have been produced by a conforming encoder. The reference implementation surfaces this rejection as <code>Error::MalformedPayloadPadding</code>; cross-implementations MUST surface a semantically equivalent rejection — distinguishable from generic checksum failure and from generic bytecode-parse failure — so that conformance tests can pin the error path.

===Bytecode layer===

====Bytecode header====

The first byte of the bytecode payload is the MK bytecode header:

{| class="wikitable"
! Bits !! Field !! Notes
|-
| 7–4 || Version || <code>0x0</code> in v0.1. Decoders MUST reject unknown versions.
|-
| 3 || Reserved || MUST be 0 in v0.1.
|-
| 2 || Fingerprint flag || <code>1</code> if <code>origin_fingerprint</code> is present in the payload; <code>0</code> if omitted.
|-
| 1 || Reserved || MUST be 0 in v0.1.
|-
| 0 || Reserved || MUST be 0 in v0.1.
|}

Valid v0.1 header values: <code>0x00</code> (no fingerprint) and <code>0x04</code> (fingerprint present).

MK's bytecode header mirrors MD's bit-allocation '''shape''' — 4-bit version field plus 4 flag/reserved bits — and shares '''bit-2 semantics''' ("optional fingerprint-related block follows"). Each format's specific block contents differ (MD: 5N-byte fingerprints block, one fp per master; MK: 4-byte <code>origin_fingerprint</code>); the bit-level convention is shared to enable a common header-parsing helper when the eventual <code>mc-codex32</code> shared crate extracts.

The two formats' '''specific bit allocations diverge from bit 3 onward''' — MK and MD make independent decisions about which flag bits each format needs. As of mk-codec v0.2.0 / md-codec v0.10.0:

* '''MK''' (this format) reserves bits 0, 1, 3 (encoders MUST emit zero; decoders MUST reject non-zero). Bit 2 is the optional-fingerprint flag.
* '''MD''' reserves bits 0, 1 (zero), uses bit 2 as the optional-fingerprint flag (shape-shared with MK), and reclaimed bit 3 as the OriginPaths flag in [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/releases/tag/md-codec-v0.10.0 md-codec v0.10.0] (1 = per-<code>@N</code> origin-path block via <code>Tag::OriginPaths = 0x36</code>; 0 = shared-path declaration via <code>Tag::SharedPath = 0x34</code>).

The shape-shared property survives the divergence: a future <code>mc-codex32</code> shared parser can extract the 4-bit version and pass the 4 flag bits back to each format's higher-level layer for format-specific interpretation. The bit-2 fingerprint-flag convention remains shared between MK and MD; bits 0, 1, 3 are independent allocations.

====Payload field order====

After the bytecode header, the payload encodes fields in the order:

<pre>
  [stub_count        : 1 byte; MUST be ≥ 1]
  [policy_id_stub_1 ... policy_id_stub_N : 4 × N bytes]
  [origin_fingerprint: 4 bytes]   ← present iff bytecode_header bit 2 set
  [origin_path       : 1 B std-table indicator OR 2..=52 B explicit-path escape]
  [xpub_compact      : 73 bytes]
</pre>

Rationale (recovery-friendly):

* Stubs first → a recovery tool scanning many cards can fast-filter by Policy ID before parsing the rest.
* Origin fingerprint + path next → matches BIP 380 origin notation <code>[fp/path]</code> reading order.
* xpub last → fixed-size, end-of-payload, simplest streaming-parser shape.

====Policy ID stubs====

Each stub is the top 4 bytes (32 bits) of an MD-encoded policy's '''WalletPolicyId''' (<code>md_codec::compute_wallet_policy_id(descriptor)</code>, md SPEC v0.13 §5.3) — '''not''' the md1 bytecode hash <code>SHA-256(canonical_bytecode)</code>, which is encoding-sensitive (see the naming note, audit I1). The stub is an indexing aid — not a cryptographic primitive — so 4 bytes is sufficient.

Birthday-bound collision probability among <code>k = 50</code> stubs at 32 bits: <code>P(collision) ≈ k(k − 1) / (2 · 2³²) ≈ 2.85 × 10⁻⁷</code> ≈ 0.00003% — effectively zero for the threat model. Even 24 bits clears it; 32 bits is conservative margin.

<code>stub_count</code> is a single byte, supporting 1–255 stubs. Practical limit is set by chunk-payload capacity. Decoders MUST reject <code>stub_count == 0</code> with <code>Error::InvalidPolicyIdStubCount</code>.

A cosigner participating in multiple wallets MAY stamp multiple Policy ID stubs on a single MK card OR engrave separate MK cards per wallet. The stubs are interpreted as an inclusive set: "this xpub is intended to serve any of the listed wallets."

====Origin fingerprint====

The 4-byte BIP 32 master fingerprint, identifying the seed from which the xpub was derived. Verbatim from the BIP 380 origin-notation <code>[fp/...]</code> prefix.

'''Present only if bytecode-header bit 2 is set; otherwise omitted from the payload entirely.''' Encoders MUST set the fingerprint flag if and only if <code>origin_fingerprint</code> is present in the payload. The invariant is structurally undetectable at decode time — no length prefix lets the decoder distinguish "flag set, fp present" from "flag unset, fp omitted" — so it is enforced as an encoder-side contract; decoders follow the flag verbatim. A hand-crafted bytecode that violates the invariant decodes to a wrong-but-internally-consistent <code>KeyCard</code>; detection happens at the §"Linkage to MD and recovery flow" Wallet Instance ID check. See §"Decoder validity rules" for the full statement of the encoder-side invariant.

The optional-fingerprint mode supports cosigners who want maximum privacy at the per-card level. See §"Privacy considerations" for guidance.

====Origin path encoding====

Indicator-byte standard-table dictionary plus a <code>0xFE</code> explicit-path escape hatch. The dictionary is '''mk1-internal''' (see "Path dictionary divergence note" below).

'''Case A — standard-table indicator''' (1 byte total). The indicator byte names a path drawn from the mk1-internal table below:

{| class="wikitable"
! Indicator !! Path !! BIP family
|-
| <code>0x01</code> || <code>m/44'/0'/0'</code> || BIP 44 mainnet
|-
| <code>0x02</code> || <code>m/49'/0'/0'</code> || BIP 49 mainnet
|-
| <code>0x03</code> || <code>m/84'/0'/0'</code> || BIP 84 mainnet
|-
| <code>0x04</code> || <code>m/86'/0'/0'</code> || BIP 86 mainnet
|-
| <code>0x05</code> || <code>m/48'/0'/0'/2'</code> || BIP 48 segwit-v0 multisig mainnet
|-
| <code>0x06</code> || <code>m/48'/0'/0'/1'</code> || BIP 48 nested-segwit multisig mainnet
|-
| <code>0x07</code> || <code>m/87'/0'/0'</code> || BIP 87 multisig mainnet
|-
| <code>0x11</code> || <code>m/44'/1'/0'</code> || BIP 44 testnet
|-
| <code>0x12</code> || <code>m/49'/1'/0'</code> || BIP 49 testnet
|-
| <code>0x13</code> || <code>m/84'/1'/0'</code> || BIP 84 testnet
|-
| <code>0x14</code> || <code>m/86'/1'/0'</code> || BIP 86 testnet
|-
| <code>0x15</code> || <code>m/48'/1'/0'/2'</code> || BIP 48 segwit-v0 multisig testnet
|-
| <code>0x16</code> || <code>m/48'/1'/0'/1'</code> || BIP 48 nested-segwit multisig testnet (added in mk-codec v0.2.0)
|-
| <code>0x17</code> || <code>m/87'/1'/0'</code> || BIP 87 multisig testnet
|}

Mainnet entries occupy <code>0x01</code>..<code>0x07</code>; testnet entries occupy <code>0x11</code>..<code>0x17</code> (the upper-nibble bit identifies testnet). The 14-entry table above is the canonical source of truth and is mk1-internal — future entries are an mk1-side decision. Reference-implementation cross-link: [https://github.com/bg002h/mnemonic-key/blob/main/crates/mk-codec/src/bytecode/path.rs <code>crates/mk-codec/src/bytecode/path.rs::STANDARD_PATHS</code>].

'''Path dictionary divergence note (v0.2.2):''' earlier drafts of this BIP described this table as mirroring MD's <code>Tag::SharedPath</code> table byte-for-byte under a path-dictionary-mirror-stewardship contract. md-codec v0.10.0 (with <code>Tag::OriginPaths = 0x36</code>) carried a compatible table; md-codec v0.11 then dropped path dictionaries from md1 entirely as part of the v0.11 wire-format cleanup (per [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/blob/main/design/SPEC_v0_11_wire_format.md <code>descriptor-mnemonic/design/SPEC_v0_11_wire_format.md</code> §1.4]). As of mk-codec v0.2.2, mk1's standard-table dictionary is '''standalone''' — mk1-internal, not a sibling mirror.

Note on indicator <code>0x16</code> (history): mk1 v0.1.x reserved <code>0x16</code> awaiting alignment with md1's then-corresponding dictionary entry. md-codec v0.9.0 / v0.10.x carried a compatible table; mk-codec v0.2.0 added <code>(0x16, m/48'/1'/0'/1')</code> at that time. The change is wire-additive: v0.1.x decoders reject <code>0x16</code> with <code>Error::InvalidPathIndicator(0x16)</code>; v0.2+ decoders accept and resolve to the BIP 48 testnet nested-segwit path. md-codec v0.11+ has since dropped path dictionaries entirely (see divergence note above).

'''Case B — explicit-path escape hatch''' (variable length), marked by indicator <code>0xFE</code>:

<pre>
  [0xFE]
  [component_count: 1 byte; MUST be in 0..=10]
  [component_1 ... component_N: each LEB128-encoded u32, hardened-bit in MSB per BIP 32]
</pre>

Path-component cap: '''10''' (closure Q-3 lock). BIP 32 itself allows depth ≤ 255; real wallets use ≤ 6. The 10-component cap bounds chunk-size attacks without rejecting any plausibly real path. Decoders MUST reject <code>component_count > 10</code> with <code>Error::PathTooDeep</code>; they MUST '''accept''' <code>component_count == 0</code>.

<code>component_count == 0</code> is the '''no-path / depth-0''' case: a root or non-HD key that has no derivation path (e.g. an xpub built from a raw WIF, or a master key). As of mk-codec v0.4.0 this is a valid card. An empty path carries zero on-wire component bytes (the escape is exactly the two bytes <code>0xFE 0x00</code>), and the decoder reconstructs <code>depth = 0</code> and <code>child_number = Normal{0}</code> (the BIP 32 master convention) — see §"xpub encoding (compact-73)". A card whose <code>origin_path</code> maps to a standard-table indicator never uses the escape at all, so <code>component_count == 0</code> only ever arises via the <code>0xFE</code> explicit form.

LEB128 of a u32 takes 1–5 bytes. Any u32 with bit 28 or higher set (which includes every hardened component, since the hardened-marker is bit 31) requires the full 5 bytes; non-hardened components < 2²⁸ take 1–4 bytes. For typical BIP-style paths (which are mostly hardened), 5 bytes per component is the typical case as well as the worst-case bound.

Indicators <code>0x00</code>, <code>0x08</code>–<code>0x10</code>, <code>0x18</code>–<code>0xFD</code>, and <code>0xFF</code> are reserved and MUST NOT be emitted by encoders. Decoders MUST reject reserved indicator bytes (<code>Error::InvalidPathIndicator</code>).

====xpub encoding (compact-73)====

MK v0.1 encodes xpubs in a 73-byte compact form:

<pre>
  [xpub.version          : 4 bytes]   — 0x0488B21E (mainnet xpub) or 0x043587CF (testnet tpub)
  [xpub.parent_fingerprint: 4 bytes]
  [xpub.chain_code       : 32 bytes]
  [xpub.public_key       : 33 bytes] — compressed secp256k1
                            ─────
                            73 bytes
</pre>

'''Accepted wire version bytes.''' The 4-byte <code>xpub.version</code> field on the wire MUST be one of exactly two values: <code>0x0488B21E</code> (mainnet <code>xpub</code>) or <code>0x043587CF</code> (testnet <code>tpub</code>). Any other value MUST be rejected with <code>Error::InvalidXpubVersion</code>. Note: this constrains the '''wire''' only. The reference <code>mk-cli</code> ''normalizes'' SLIP-0132 prefixes (<code>ypub</code>/<code>zpub</code>/<code>upub</code>/<code>vpub</code>/…) to the canonical BIP 32 prefix on '''input''' (FOLLOWUP <code>mk-slip0132-prefix-acceptance</code>); the wire itself never carries a SLIP-0132 version, so this is not an "encoders reject all others" rule at the CLI surface — it is a wire-format constraint.

The fields <code>xpub.depth</code> and <code>xpub.child_number</code> are absent from the wire and reconstructed at decode time from <code>origin_path</code>:

<pre>
  depth        := component_count(origin_path)
  child_number := last_component(origin_path) including hardened-bit encoding,
                  or Normal{0} when origin_path is empty (depth-0 / no-path key)
</pre>

For a standard-table indicator, both come from the dictionary entry. For the explicit-path escape hatch, both come from the on-wire components. For the '''empty path''' (the <code>0xFE 0x00</code> no-path / depth-0 case, e.g. a WIF), <code>depth = 0</code> and <code>child_number</code> defaults to the BIP 32 master convention <code>Normal{0}</code>; this reconstruction is the exact inverse of the encoder-side invariant below.

'''Why compact-73:''' the dropped fields (<code>depth</code>, <code>child_number</code>) are redundant with <code>origin_path</code>; carrying them on-wire risks drift detected by md1-style depth-mismatch logic. Compact-73 is ''lossless'' — both fields are reconstructible from the path — and saves 5 bytes per card (~one row of typical hand-engraving). Because <code>depth</code> and <code>child_number</code> are not carried on the wire, ''on-wire'' drift (a card that decodes to two different depth/child readings) is impossible by construction. Drift between the '''supplied xpub''' and the '''origin path''' is a different, real hazard, closed by an encoder-side invariant:

'''Encoder-side invariant (compact-73 consistency).''' Because the decoder reconstructs <code>depth</code>/<code>child_number</code> solely from <code>origin_path</code>, an encoder MUST reject any card whose supplied <code>xpub.depth</code> or <code>xpub.child_number</code> disagrees with <code>origin_path</code>. Concretely, let <code>path_depth = component_count(origin_path)</code> and <code>expected_child = last_component(origin_path)</code>, or <code>Normal{0}</code> when <code>origin_path</code> is empty (the depth-0 / no-path case). The encoder MUST reject with <code>Error::XpubOriginPathMismatch</code> unless <code>xpub.depth == path_depth</code> '''and''' <code>xpub.child_number == expected_child</code>. Equivalently: a card encodes iff it survives the compact-drop and reconstruction unchanged. This check is '''not''' a decoder rule — the disagreement is structurally undetectable at decode time (no on-wire depth to compare against) — so it is an encoder contract. A hand-crafted bytecode that violates it decodes to a wrong-but-internally-consistent xpub whose reconstructed 78-byte serialization then fails the Wallet Instance ID check at recovery (§"Linkage to MD and recovery flow"), or silently imports wrong metadata where no external anchor exists.

'''Limit-of-detection note (carries into §"Privacy considerations"):''' under compact-73, an operator who picks the wrong standard-table path indicator while engraving the right xpub bytes produces a card that decodes without error and reconstructs an xpub claiming the wrong derivation path. The card's <code>chain_code</code> and <code>public_key</code> are correct (addresses derive correctly), but the path embedded in the reconstructed BIP 32 serialization is wrong. Detection of this class of error happens at the §"Linkage to MD and recovery flow" Wallet Instance ID check — only when the user has an externally-anchored expected wallet identity to compare against. The §"Privacy considerations" section recommends out-of-band first-address verification as a mitigation.

A future MK version MAY allocate a "compact-65" mode (further dropping <code>xpub.version</code> and <code>xpub.parent_fingerprint</code>) in the bytecode header's reserved bits if engraving cost ever proves binding. Compact-65 is lossy (<code>parent_fingerprint</code> is not reconstructible from on-card data); compact-73 is the lossless v0.1 baseline.

===Decoder validity rules===

A conformant MK decoder MUST reject input that:

# Has a bytecode header with version != 0 in v0.1 (<code>Error::UnsupportedVersion</code>).
# Has any reserved bit set in the bytecode header in v0.1 (<code>Error::ReservedBitsSet</code>).
# Has <code>stub_count == 0</code> (<code>Error::InvalidPolicyIdStubCount</code>).
# Has an origin path indicator outside the defined table or in the reserved ranges (<code>Error::InvalidPathIndicator</code>).
# Has an explicit path with <code>component_count > 10</code> (<code>Error::PathTooDeep</code>). <code>component_count == 0</code> is '''valid''' (the no-path / depth-0 case, mk-codec v0.4.0+); only <code>> 10</code> is rejected.
# Has any path component with the BIP 32 child-number high bits set in invalid ways, or LEB128 overflow (<code>Error::InvalidPathComponent</code>).
# Has xpub version bytes outside the accepted wire set <code>{0x0488B21E (mainnet xpub), 0x043587CF (testnet tpub)}</code> (<code>Error::InvalidXpubVersion</code>). SLIP-0132 prefixes (ypub/zpub/…) never appear on the wire; the reference mk-cli normalizes them to the canonical BIP 32 prefix on input (see §"xpub encoding (compact-73)").
# Has xpub <code>public_key</code> bytes that do not parse as a valid compressed secp256k1 point (<code>Error::InvalidXpubPublicKey</code>). Unreachable for inputs that pass BCH verification, but required for hand-constructed inputs fed directly to the bytecode decoder.
# Truncates anywhere mid-field (<code>Error::UnexpectedEnd</code>).
# Has trailing bytes after the xpub (<code>Error::TrailingBytes</code>).

Encoder-side invariant (not a decoder rule): encoders MUST set the bytecode-header fingerprint flag (bit 2) if and only if <code>origin_fingerprint</code> is present in the payload. The invariant is structurally undetectable at decode time (no length prefix distinguishes "flag set, fp present" from "flag unset, fp omitted"); decoders follow the flag verbatim. A hand-crafted bytecode that violates the invariant decodes to a wrong-but-internally-consistent <code>KeyCard</code>; detection happens at the §"Linkage to MD and recovery flow" Wallet Instance ID check. (No dedicated <code>Error</code> variant: this rule is an encoder contract, not a decoder reject path.)

Per-string BCH / string-layer decoder rules (applied to each input string before reassembly):

# Missing HRP, or an HRP other than <code>mk</code> (<code>Error::InvalidHrp</code>).
# Mixed upper/lower case within a single string (<code>Error::MixedCase</code>); the HRP case MUST match the data-part case.
# A data-part character outside the bech32 alphabet (<code>Error::InvalidChar</code>).
# A data-part length outside the valid BCH envelope — below '''14''', in the reserved '''94–95''' gap, or above 108 (<code>Error::InvalidStringLength</code>). The minimum valid data-part length is 14 symbols; totals 94–95 are reserved-invalid to keep code-variant selection unambiguous (§"Checksum", code selection).
# A checksum that cannot be repaired within the BCH <code>t = 4</code> substitution radius (<code>Error::BchUncorrectable</code>).
# Empty input, or a declared-chunked string too short to carry its 8-symbol header (<code>Error::ChunkedHeaderMalformed</code>).
# An assembled bytecode exceeding the chunk-capacity bound <code>32 × 53 − 4 = 1692</code> bytes (<code>Error::CardPayloadTooLarge</code>).

Additional decoder rules at the string/chunking layer:

# Chunk-header card-type byte not in <code>{0x00, 0x01}</code> (<code>Error::UnsupportedCardType</code>).
# For multi-string input: the supplied list mixes <code>SingleString</code> and <code>Chunked</code> header variants (e.g., a <code>SingleString</code> followed by additional strings, or a <code>Chunked</code> set with one or more <code>SingleString</code> entries interleaved); such inputs MUST be rejected (<code>Error::MixedHeaderTypes</code>). Distinct from <code>ChunkedHeaderMalformed</code>, which covers issues ''within'' a declared-chunked set.
# For chunked input: chunks share a common <code>chunk_set_id</code>; mismatched values MUST be rejected (<code>Error::ChunkSetIdMismatch</code>).
# For chunked input: <code>chunk_index</code> values cover <code>0..total_chunks</code> exactly once; gaps, duplicates, or out-of-range values MUST be rejected (<code>Error::ChunkedHeaderMalformed</code>).
# For chunked input: reassembled bytecode's trailing <code>cross_chunk_hash</code> MUST equal <code>SHA-256(canonical_bytecode)[0..4]</code> (<code>Error::CrossChunkHashMismatch</code>).
# 5-bit payload symbols, after BCH verification, MUST byte-align (<code>Error::MalformedPayloadPadding</code>).

Note: an earlier draft had a decoder rule rejecting <code>xpub.depth</code>-vs-path drift. Under the locked compact-73 form, <code>xpub.depth</code> and <code>xpub.child_number</code> are not carried on the wire, so a decoder has nothing to compare and ''on-wire'' drift is impossible by construction — hence no decoder rule. The related hazard — a supplied xpub whose depth/child disagrees with <code>origin_path</code> — is closed at encode time by the '''encoder-side invariant''' in §"xpub encoding (compact-73)" (<code>Error::XpubOriginPathMismatch</code>), not by a decoder reject path.

===Linkage to MD and recovery flow===

An MK card with policy-ID stubs <code>[stub_1, ..., stub_N]</code> declares: "this xpub is intended to serve any MD-encoded policy whose Policy ID stub matches one of these stubs." Each stub is the top 4 bytes of a '''form-aware''' md1 identity — '''not''' the encoding-sensitive md1 bytecode hash <code>SHA-256(canonical_bytecode)</code> (superseded; see the naming note, audit I1):

* For a '''keyed''' wallet-policy md1 card, the stub is the top 4 bytes of the '''WalletPolicyId''' (<code>md_codec::compute_wallet_policy_id(descriptor)</code>, md SPEC v0.13 §5.3).
* For a '''keyless template''' md1 card (the <code>@N</code>-placeholder template form, no concrete cosigner keys), the stub is the top 4 bytes of the '''WalletDescriptorTemplateId''' — the key-stable template identity.

The orchestrator computes the candidate stub from whichever md1 form it decoded (matching the reference <code>mk-cli</code> <code>derive_stub_from_md1</code> / the toolkit's <code>bundle_binding_stub</code>, toolkit #28) and compares it against the MK card's stubs.

Recovery flow (the canonical sequence wallet software SHOULD follow):

# Decode the MD policy card. Compute its full 16-byte identity — the '''WalletPolicyId''' (<code>md_codec::compute_wallet_policy_id(descriptor)</code>) for a keyed wallet-policy card, or the '''WalletDescriptorTemplateId''' for a keyless template card. Take the top 4 bytes as <code>policy_stub</code>.
# For each candidate MK card:
## Decode and extract its Policy ID stubs.
## Reject the card unless <code>policy_stub</code> matches one of its stubs.
# For each accepted MK card, plug its xpub into the corresponding <code>@N</code> slot in the policy template (matched by origin path, by user assignment, or by the cosigner-name metadata that future MK versions may carry).
# Reconstruct the full descriptor with concrete xpubs. Compute the Wallet Instance ID = <code>SHA-256(canonical_bytecode || canonical_xpub_serialization)[0..16]</code> where <code>canonical_xpub_serialization</code> is the concatenation of each <code>@N</code>-placeholder's resolved 78-byte xpub serialization in placeholder-index order. Verify the Wallet Instance ID matches the wallet the user expected to recover (e.g., compared against a separately-anchored Wallet Instance ID phrase, or against a digital backup record).
# Reject if mismatch. Accept and proceed to address derivation if match.

Step 2 is the indexing aid — fast filter, template-level. Step 4 is the cryptographic per-instance check. Both are required for safe recovery.

====Authority precedence (MK ↔ MD path information)====

MD's wire format optionally carries per-<code>@N</code> origin paths (allocated to <code>Tag::OriginPaths = 0x36</code> in [https://github.com/bg002h/descriptor-mnemonic/releases/tag/md-codec-v0.10.0 md-codec v0.10.0]; see MD's BIP §"Per-<code>@N</code> path declaration"). When both an MK card and an MD card with per-<code>@N</code> paths participate in recovery for the same wallet:

* MK's <code>origin_path</code> is '''authoritative''' for the xpub's derivation. (It is the actual provenance of the xpub on the card.)
* MD's per-<code>@N</code> path is the policy's ''expected'' path. (It is descriptive — the slot's intended derivation shape.)

Mismatch MUST cause '''the recovery orchestrator''' to reject the assembly. Per-format decoders are not required to be aware of cross-format context; the cross-format consistency check belongs to the orchestrator layer that sits above both decoders. Implementations MUST surface a precise error identifying both the policy-side expected path and the key-side actual path so the user can diagnose which card's path information is wrong.

==Privacy considerations==

An MK card alone is a strictly larger privacy footprint than the corresponding MD policy card.

A lost or photographed MK card reveals:

* The xpub → full transaction history (every receive/change address) for that account is reconstructable by anyone with the bitcoin chain.
* The origin fingerprint (when present) → identifies the master seed (a 4-byte fingerprint is weak as a unique identifier but sufficient as a hint for an adversary cross-referencing other leaks). Optional in v0.1; see below.
* The origin path → identifies the BIP-style account family.
* One or more Policy IDs → identifies which MD-encoded wallets this xpub serves.

By contrast, an MD policy card alone reveals only the policy structure (template) and optionally key fingerprints (with the existing MD privacy warning); it does not enable transaction-history reconstruction.

Recommendations:

* '''Storage discipline.''' MK cards SHOULD be stored with the same physical security as a seed backup (safety-deposit box, fire safe, etc.).
* '''No photography.''' MK cards MUST NOT be photographed. The xpub's full transaction history is one OCR away.
* '''Stub-count tradeoff.''' Cosigners participating in multiple wallets have a privacy tradeoff in the Policy ID stub count: stamping multiple stubs on one MK card means recovery for any of those wallets reveals (to the recoverer) that the cosigner is in all of them. Cosigners who care about cross-wallet privacy SHOULD stick to one Policy ID stub per card and engrave additional cards.
* '''Optional fingerprint engraving.''' Cosigners who do not need their MK card to carry master-seed identification (e.g., when the policy card already carries fingerprints, or when <code>@N</code> assignment is performed out-of-band) MAY engrave with the fingerprint flag unset, omitting <code>origin_fingerprint</code>. Encoders SHOULD expose this as an explicit choice rather than a default.
* '''Disposal of rotated cards.''' When an xpub is rotated to a new master seed, the corresponding MK card SHOULD be physically destroyed. Engraved-on-steel cards that are no longer cryptographically active still carry the same privacy footprint as active cards (full transaction history reconstructable).
* '''Hand-off discipline.''' An MK card transferred to a wallet creator at provisioning time carries the same per-card privacy footprint as one read at recovery time. Cosigners SHOULD treat the act of hand-off — including any photograph, scan, or transcription that occurs during it — with the same operational discipline as they would the card itself in long-term storage.
* '''Integrity detection limit.''' Under the locked compact-73 xpub form (see §"xpub encoding (compact-73)"), an operator who selects the wrong standard-table path indicator while engraving the correct xpub bytes produces a card that decodes without error but reconstructs an xpub claiming the wrong derivation path. Detection of this class of error happens at the Wallet Instance ID check (§"Linkage to MD and recovery flow" step 4), which requires the user to hold an externally-anchored expected wallet identity. Cosigners performing single-wallet recoveries without an external anchor SHOULD verify the first address derived from the reconstructed xpub against an independently-recorded expected address before proceeding with funds movement.

==Rationale==

===Why a separate format from MD?===

An xpub backup format is independently useful: watch-only wallet provisioning, master-public-key archival, key-rotation continuity. None of these needs the descriptor layer. Coupling MK to MD's release cadence is artificial. Separate BIPs let each spec target its own readers and evolve on its own timeline.

===Why per-cosigner atomic cards instead of bundled xpubs?===

A bundle card with N xpubs is denser on the wire but creates a single point of failure: lose the bundle = lose every cosigner's xpub. Per-cosigner cards align with the trust boundary (a cosigner can hand their card to the wallet creator without revealing other cosigners) and allow xpub reuse across wallets.

===Why ≥1 Policy ID stubs (not zero)?===

Anonymous MK cards (zero stubs) maximize privacy and reusability but eliminate the recovery indexing flow. The cryptographic check at step 4 of recovery is not affected by the stub count, but the search-for-matching-cards step at step 2 falls back to "try every MK card in every <code>@N</code> slot until the Wallet Instance ID matches" — workable but ergonomically poor. Requiring ≥1 stub gives a default fast-filter; cosigners who care about cross-wallet privacy can still choose to stamp only one.

===Why reuse BIP 93's BCH polynomial?===

BCH generator polynomials are not cryptographic secrets. They are chosen for minimum-distance and weight-distribution properties that bound error-correction guarantees. Reusing BIP 93's polynomial preserves codex32's error-correction guarantees without an independent polynomial search. HRP-mixing and per-format target residues provide sufficient cross-format domain separation. This is the same pattern bech32 uses for <code>bc</code> / <code>tb</code> / <code>bcrt</code> across [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0173.mediawiki BIP 173] and [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0350.mediawiki BIP 350].

===Why compact-73 instead of full 78-byte xpub?===

The dropped fields <code>xpub.depth</code> and <code>xpub.child_number</code> are derivable from <code>origin_path</code>, which the format already carries authoritatively. Carrying them on-wire is redundant and introduces a drift class that compact-73 eliminates by construction. The 5-byte saving is small but recurring (every card pays it). A more aggressive compact-65 (further dropping <code>xpub.version</code> and <code>xpub.parent_fingerprint</code>) was considered and rejected for v0.1: <code>parent_fingerprint</code> is not reconstructible from on-card data, and the v0.1 threat model does not justify the loss. Future versions may revisit if engraving cost ever becomes binding.

===Why path-component cap = 10?===

BIP 32 itself allows depth ≤ 255; real BIP-style derivations top out at 6 (BIP 48 multisig is 4). The 10-component cap allows a comfortable margin for any plausibly real path while bounding the worst-case malicious-explicit-path inflation to a small number of bytes. Tighter caps are strictly better for a sanity gate — they don't lock out real users and they bound the worst-case inflation more aggressively.

===Why an optional fingerprint flag?===

The fingerprint reveals master-seed identity, which links the xpub on this card to other xpubs from the same master. Cosigners may not need that linkage carried on the MK card if it's already on the policy card or available out-of-band. The bytecode header's bit 2 (mirroring MD's bit-2 fingerprint flag) lets encoders elide the field; decoders handle both modes without ambiguity since the flag and payload presence MUST agree.

==Frequently Asked Questions==

===Why not just use a Coldcard <code>.txt</code> multisig config?===

Coldcard's multisig config is ASCII text, not BCH-checksummed. Engraving it on steel is feasible but the recovery story is "transcribe by hand and hope you got every character right." MK gives you BCH error-correction guarantees: up to 4 character substitutions per string recoverable in '''both''' the regular and long codes (8 is the long code's error-detection radius, not correction). For durable physical media that may degrade or be partially obscured, that's a meaningful difference.

===Why not embed xpubs in the MD policy card?===

Each xpub is 78 bytes. A 7-cosigner wallet adds ~550 bytes of payload to the policy card, blowing past single-string capacity into multi-chunk encoding for every wallet. MK keeps the policy card compact (template only) and pushes xpub bytes onto separate per-cosigner cards that are independently useful and reusable.

===Why HRP <code>mk</code> specifically?===

Alphabetical extension of the codex32-family namespace already established by BIP 93's <code>ms</code> (mnemonic seed share) and MD's <code>md</code> (mnemonic descriptor). The mnemonic device is "mnemonic key." No known collisions with BIP 173 / BIP 350 segwit address HRPs, BOLT 11 / BOLT 12 Lightning HRPs, or in-flight bitcoin-dev BIP proposals as of the draft date. A formal SLIP-0173 collision check is required before formal BIP submission. Alternatives <code>mx</code>, <code>mkc</code>, <code>mpk</code> are documented should <code>mk</code> turn out to be contested.

===What about xprv (extended private key) backup?===

Out of scope. Backing up secret material is BIP 93's job; codex32 was designed for exactly that. MK is for public material only.

===What about MuSig2 aggregate keys?===

Out of scope for v0.1. MuSig2 (BIP 327) aggregates cosigner keys into a single point; the resulting aggregate isn't a BIP 32 xpub and doesn't fit MK's payload shape. A future MK version or sibling format may address this.

===Can MK cards be reused across wallets?===

Yes — that's a primary design goal. A single cosigner's xpub backed up on one MK card can serve every wallet that cosigner participates in. Cosigners can stamp multiple Policy ID stubs on one card to declare which wallets it serves, or use a single stub and engrave additional cards. See §"Privacy considerations" for the cross-wallet-leakage tradeoff.

===Why not just engrave the BIP 32 xpub Base58Check string directly?===

A Base58Check xpub is ~111 ASCII characters, with no error correction. MK's codex32-derived encoding is similar length but adds BCH error correction (up to 4 character substitutions recoverable, for both the regular and long code variants), HRP-mixed domain separation against accidental cross-format validation, and a structured payload that includes origin metadata and Policy ID stubs without a separate label.

==Backwards Compatibility==

This is a new format with no prior versions. There are no backwards-compatibility concerns within MK itself.

MK is designed to coexist with MD: the two formats share the codex32 BCH plumbing (same polynomials, same alphabet), but each has its own HRP, its own NUMS-derived target constants, and its own bytecode-layer payload structure. A decoder receiving a string MUST dispatch by HRP and reject any string whose HRP does not match the format the decoder implements.

==Test Vectors==

The conformance corpus ships with the v0.1 reference implementation at <code>crates/mk-codec/src/test_vectors/v0.1.json</code> (surfaced by <code>mk vectors</code>). It follows the schema convention used by MD: a JSON file listing '''positive''' vectors (canonical encode/decode round-trips) and '''negative''' vectors (one per <code>Error</code> variant the decoder can produce).

Each positive vector pins, at minimum:

* the per-chunk <code>mk1</code> strings, in <code>chunk_index</code> order;
* the <code>canonical_bytecode_hex</code> (the deterministic pre-chunking payload);
* the deterministic <code>chunk_set_id</code> and <code>total_chunks</code>;
* the decoder correction state (e.g. <code>clean</code>).

The corpus covers 1-stub and multi-stub cards, mainnet and testnet (<code>tpub</code>), fingerprint-present and privacy (no-fingerprint) modes, standard-table and explicit-path origins, multi-chunk cards (including 3-chunk cards), and — added in this cycle — a '''depth-0 / no-path''' card exercising the <code>component_count == 0</code> rule (§"Origin path encoding"). Vector <code>V1_bip48_mainnet_1_stub_with_fp</code> is the card walked through in §"Checksum" above. The negative table pins one reject per decoder-reachable <code>Error</code> variant.

The corpus is anchored by a family-stable SHA-256 under a token of the form <code>mk-codec X.Y</code>. '''Patch'''-version bumps preserve the byte-stable SHA; '''minor'''-version bumps roll the token. (Honesty note: the token tracks the decode-family, not the crate patch level. The v0.3 and v0.4 minor bumps '''missed''' their token rolls; this cycle corrects the token to <code>mk-codec 0.4</code> in the same regeneration that adds the depth-0 vector — that card decodes only under mk-codec v0.4.0+, so the corpus must not carry an older family token.) The exact pinned SHA-256 and the embedded per-vector values are those of the reference corpus as regenerated in this cycle.

A pre-submission '''Error-variant ↔ negative-vector parity audit''' is required before formal BIP submission: every reject case in §"Decoder validity rules" MUST map to a uniquely-named <code>Error</code> variant in the reference crate, and every variant MUST have at least one planned negative test vector. This mirrors md-codec's 30-negative-vectors-one-per-Error-variant conformance contract.

==Reference Implementation==

<code>mk-codec</code> Rust crate at [https://github.com/bg002h/mnemonic-key <code>github.com/bg002h/mnemonic-key</code>] in <code>crates/mk-codec/</code>. Initial scaffold forks BCH primitives from <code>md-codec</code>. The shared codex32-derived plumbing extracts to a <code>mc-codex32</code> crate (likely a third sibling repo) once both formats are implementation-validated; the trigger condition is "both md-codec and mk-codec at v1.0 with cross-validated conformance vectors." Until then, fork-from-md-codec; both implementations carry their own copy of the BCH primitives.

